==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*swīkaną}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term/t|ofs|swīka||stay far from}}, Old Saxon {{term/t|osx|swīkan||betray, languish}}, Middle Dutch {{term/t|dum|swiken}} (Dutch {{term/t|nl|bezwijken||give way, sink}}), Old High German {{term/t|goh|swīhhan}} (dialectical German {{term/t|de|schweichen||wander round, deceive}}), Old Norse {{term/t|non|svíkva||betray}} (Swedish {{term/t|sv|svika}}, Danish {{term/t|da|svige}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈswiːkɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|head=swīcan}}

# to [[deceive]], be treacherous

